    {
      "id": "grafana.candidate.f5971b3e4cc960977e17",
      "syntaxRole": "declarative-or-imperative-paragraph",
      "sourceText": "[Grafana Alerting](/docs/grafana/latest/alerting/) represents a powerful new approach to systems observability and incident response management. While the alerting platform is perhaps best known for its strong integrations with Prometheus, the system works with numerous popular data sources including InfluxDB. In this tutorial we will learn how to create Grafana alerts using InfluxDB and the newer Flux query language. We will cover five common scenarios from the most basic to the most complex. Together, these five scenarios will provide an excellent guide for almost any type of alerting query that you wish to create using Grafana and Flux.",
      "headingPath": [
        "How to create Grafana alerts with InfluxDB and the Flux query language"
      ],
      "source": {
        "path": "docs/sources/tutorials/create-alerts-from-flux-queries/index.md",
        "gitBlobSha": "ff67f50b26681d3dc7d4d8e7eb10e4620c9488c3",
        "sha256": "48b344a4b7311941040db4785da4177ce4cd0dcaccadd5ebdd24c843c328bc40",
        "lineStart": 23,
        "lineEnd": 23,
        "url": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/grafana/grafana/6193dc03311b631b9727b560d24369e683dc396e/docs/sources/tutorials/create-alerts-from-flux-queries/index.md#L23-L23"
      },
      "applicability": {
        "status": "oss-labelled-source-candidate-not-yet-reviewed",
        "products": [
          "enterprise",
          "oss"
        ]
      },
      "atomicity": "unreviewed-may-require-splitting-or-deduplication",
      "acceptanceContract": null,
      "verificationStatus": "not-tested"
    },
    {
      "id": "grafana.candidate.885e18284722afcb0ad5",
      "syntaxRole": "declarative-or-imperative-paragraph",
      "sourceText": "Before we dive into our alerting scenarios, it is worth considering the development of InfluxDB's two popular query languages: InfluxQL and Flux. Originally, InfluxDB used [InfluxQL](https://docs.influxdata.com/influxdb/v2.5/reference/syntax/influxql/spec/) as their query language, which uses a SQL-like syntax. But beginning with InfluxDB v1.8, the company introduced [Flux](https://docs.influxdata.com/flux/v0.x/), \"an open source functional data scripting language designed for querying, analyzing, and acting on data.\" \"Flux,\" its official documentation goes on to state, \"unifies code for querying, processing, writing, and acting on data into a single syntax. The language is designed to be usable, readable, flexible, composable, testable, contributable, and shareable.\"",
      "headingPath": [
        "How to create Grafana alerts with InfluxDB and the Flux query language"
      ],
      "source": {
        "path": "docs/sources/tutorials/create-alerts-from-flux-queries/index.md",
        "gitBlobSha": "ff67f50b26681d3dc7d4d8e7eb10e4620c9488c3",
        "sha256": "48b344a4b7311941040db4785da4177ce4cd0dcaccadd5ebdd24c843c328bc40",
        "lineStart": 25,
        "lineEnd": 25,
        "url": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/grafana/grafana/6193dc03311b631b9727b560d24369e683dc396e/docs/sources/tutorials/create-alerts-from-flux-queries/index.md#L25-L25"
      },
      "applicability": {
        "status": "oss-labelled-source-candidate-not-yet-reviewed",
        "products": [
          "enterprise",
          "oss"
        ]
      },
      "atomicity": "unreviewed-may-require-splitting-or-deduplication",
      "acceptanceContract": null,
      "verificationStatus": "not-tested"
    },
    {
      "id": "grafana.candidate.2ebd2cdf1c4261f9aca1",
      "syntaxRole": "declarative-or-imperative-paragraph",
      "sourceText": "In the following five examples we will see just how powerful and flexible the new Flux query language can be. We will also see just how well Flux pairs with Grafana Alerting.",
      "headingPath": [
        "How to create Grafana alerts with InfluxDB and the Flux query language"
      ],
      "source": {
        "path": "docs/sources/tutorials/create-alerts-from-flux-queries/index.md",
        "gitBlobSha": "ff67f50b26681d3dc7d4d8e7eb10e4620c9488c3",
        "sha256": "48b344a4b7311941040db4785da4177ce4cd0dcaccadd5ebdd24c843c328bc40",
        "lineStart": 27,
        "lineEnd": 27,
        "url": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/grafana/grafana/6193dc03311b631b9727b560d24369e683dc396e/docs/sources/tutorials/create-alerts-from-flux-queries/index.md#L27-L27"
      },
      "applicability": {
        "status": "oss-labelled-source-candidate-not-yet-reviewed",
        "products": [
          "enterprise",
          "oss"
        ]
      },
      "atomicity": "unreviewed-may-require-splitting-or-deduplication",
      "acceptanceContract": null,
      "verificationStatus": "not-tested"
    },
    {
      "id": "grafana.candidate.0df8ef2f7bda72cff996",
      "syntaxRole": "declarative-or-imperative-paragraph",
      "sourceText": "Our first example uses a common real-world scenario for InfluxDB and Grafana Alerting. Popular with IoT and edge applications, InfluxDB excels at on-site, real-time observability. In this example, and in fact for many of the following examples, we will consider the hypothetical scenario where we are monitoring a number of fluid tanks in a manufacturing plant. This scenario, [based on an actual application of InfluxDB and Alerting](/go/grafanaconline/2021/plant-efficiency-grafana-cloud/), will allow us to work through Grafana's various alerting setups, progressing from the simplest to the most complex.",
      "headingPath": [
        "How to create Grafana alerts with InfluxDB and the Flux query language",
        "Example 1: Create an alert when a value is above or below a set threshold"
      ],
      "source": {
        "path": "docs/sources/tutorials/create-alerts-from-flux-queries/index.md",
        "gitBlobSha": "ff67f50b26681d3dc7d4d8e7eb10e4620c9488c3",
        "sha256": "48b344a4b7311941040db4785da4177ce4cd0dcaccadd5ebdd24c843c328bc40",
        "lineStart": 31,
        "lineEnd": 31,
