        "Create a Grafana Alert rule"
      ],
      "source": {
        "path": "docs/sources/tutorials/create-alerts-from-flux-queries/index.md",
        "gitBlobSha": "ff67f50b26681d3dc7d4d8e7eb10e4620c9488c3",
        "sha256": "48b344a4b7311941040db4785da4177ce4cd0dcaccadd5ebdd24c843c328bc40",
        "lineStart": 44,
        "lineEnd": 44,
        "url": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/grafana/grafana/6193dc03311b631b9727b560d24369e683dc396e/docs/sources/tutorials/create-alerts-from-flux-queries/index.md#L44-L44"
      },
      "applicability": {
        "status": "oss-labelled-source-candidate-not-yet-reviewed",
        "products": [
          "enterprise",
          "oss"
        ]
      },
      "atomicity": "unreviewed-may-require-splitting-or-deduplication",
      "acceptanceContract": null,
      "verificationStatus": "not-tested"
    },
    {
      "id": "grafana.candidate.ada2eeab13909134e0f3",
      "syntaxRole": "declarative-or-imperative-paragraph",
      "sourceText": "Still in the **Step 2** section of the Alert rule page, you will see three boxes: a query editor (`A`), and then two sections labelled `B` and `C`. You will use these three sections to construct your rule. Let's move through them one by one.",
      "headingPath": [
        "How to create Grafana alerts with InfluxDB and the Flux query language",
        "Example 1: Create an alert when a value is above or below a set threshold",
        "Add an initial Flux query to the alert rule"
      ],
      "source": {
        "path": "docs/sources/tutorials/create-alerts-from-flux-queries/index.md",
        "gitBlobSha": "ff67f50b26681d3dc7d4d8e7eb10e4620c9488c3",
        "sha256": "48b344a4b7311941040db4785da4177ce4cd0dcaccadd5ebdd24c843c328bc40",
        "lineStart": 48,
        "lineEnd": 48,
        "url": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/grafana/grafana/6193dc03311b631b9727b560d24369e683dc396e/docs/sources/tutorials/create-alerts-from-flux-queries/index.md#L48-L48"
      },
      "applicability": {
        "status": "oss-labelled-source-candidate-not-yet-reviewed",
        "products": [
          "enterprise",
          "oss"
        ]
      },
      "atomicity": "unreviewed-may-require-splitting-or-deduplication",
      "acceptanceContract": null,
      "verificationStatus": "not-tested"
    },
    {
      "id": "grafana.candidate.f0b9452f86a89b863e58",
      "syntaxRole": "declarative-or-imperative-paragraph",
      "sourceText": "First, we want to query the data in our imaginary InfluxDB instance to obtain a time series graph of the temperature of tank A5. For this you would choose your InfluxDB data source from the dropdown and then write a query like this:",
      "headingPath": [
        "How to create Grafana alerts with InfluxDB and the Flux query language",
        "Example 1: Create an alert when a value is above or below a set threshold",
        "Add an initial Flux query to the alert rule"
      ],
      "source": {
        "path": "docs/sources/tutorials/create-alerts-from-flux-queries/index.md",
        "gitBlobSha": "ff67f50b26681d3dc7d4d8e7eb10e4620c9488c3",
        "sha256": "48b344a4b7311941040db4785da4177ce4cd0dcaccadd5ebdd24c843c328bc40",
        "lineStart": 50,
        "lineEnd": 50,
        "url": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/grafana/grafana/6193dc03311b631b9727b560d24369e683dc396e/docs/sources/tutorials/create-alerts-from-flux-queries/index.md#L50-L50"
      },
      "applicability": {
        "status": "oss-labelled-source-candidate-not-yet-reviewed",
        "products": [
          "enterprise",
          "oss"
        ]
      },
      "atomicity": "unreviewed-may-require-splitting-or-deduplication",
      "acceptanceContract": null,
      "verificationStatus": "not-tested"
    },
    {
      "id": "grafana.candidate.56bd5527ea7e3be1140b",
      "syntaxRole": "declarative-or-imperative-paragraph",
      "sourceText": "This is a fairly typical Flux query. Let's go through it function by function. We begin using [the `from()` function](https://docs.influxdata.com/flux/v0.x/stdlib/influxdata/influxdb/from/) to choose the correct bucket where our tank data resides. Then we use [a `range()` function](https://docs.influxdata.com/flux/v0.x/stdlib/universe/range/) to filter our rows based on time constraints. Then we pass our data through three [`filter()` functions](https://docs.influxdata.com/flux/v0.x/stdlib/universe/filter/) to narrow our results. We choose a specific [`measurement` (a special keyword in InfluxDB)](https://docs.influxdata.com/influxdb/v1.8/concepts/glossary/#measurement), then our tank in question (`A5`), and then a specific [`field` (another special keyword in InfluxDB)](https://docs.influxdata.com/influxdb/v1.8/concepts/glossary/#field). After this we pass the data into [an `aggregateWindow()` function](https://docs.influxdata.com/flux/v0.x/stdlib/universe/aggregatewindow/), which downsamples our data into specific periods of time, and then finally [a `yield()` function](https://docs.influxdata.com/flux/v0.x/stdlib/universe/yield/), which specifies which final result we want: `mean`.",
      "headingPath": [
        "How to create Grafana alerts with InfluxDB and the Flux query language",
        "Example 1: Create an alert when a value is above or below a set threshold",
        "Add an initial Flux query to the alert rule"
      ],
      "source": {
        "path": "docs/sources/tutorials/create-alerts-from-flux-queries/index.md",
        "gitBlobSha": "ff67f50b26681d3dc7d4d8e7eb10e4620c9488c3",
        "sha256": "48b344a4b7311941040db4785da4177ce4cd0dcaccadd5ebdd24c843c328bc40",
        "lineStart": 62,
        "lineEnd": 62,
        "url": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/grafana/grafana/6193dc03311b631b9727b560d24369e683dc396e/docs/sources/tutorials/create-alerts-from-flux-queries/index.md#L62-L62"
      },
      "applicability": {
        "status": "oss-labelled-source-candidate-not-yet-reviewed",
        "products": [
          "enterprise",
          "oss"
        ]
      },
      "atomicity": "unreviewed-may-require-splitting-or-deduplication",
      "acceptanceContract": null,
      "verificationStatus": "not-tested"
    },
    {
      "id": "grafana.candidate.fb4574492e7d5eaa16f2",
      "syntaxRole": "declarative-or-imperative-paragraph",
      "sourceText": "This Flux query will yield a time-series graph like this:",
      "headingPath": [
        "How to create Grafana alerts with InfluxDB and the Flux query language",
        "Example 1: Create an alert when a value is above or below a set threshold",
        "Add an initial Flux query to the alert rule"
      ],
      "source": {
        "path": "docs/sources/tutorials/create-alerts-from-flux-queries/index.md",
        "gitBlobSha": "ff67f50b26681d3dc7d4d8e7eb10e4620c9488c3",
        "sha256": "48b344a4b7311941040db4785da4177ce4cd0dcaccadd5ebdd24c843c328bc40",
        "lineStart": 64,
        "lineEnd": 64,
        "url": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/grafana/grafana/6193dc03311b631b9727b560d24369e683dc396e/docs/sources/tutorials/create-alerts-from-flux-queries/index.md#L64-L64"
      },
      "applicability": {
        "status": "oss-labelled-source-candidate-not-yet-reviewed",
        "products": [
          "enterprise",
          "oss"
        ]
      },
      "atomicity": "unreviewed-may-require-splitting-or-deduplication",
      "acceptanceContract": null,
      "verificationStatus": "not-tested"
    },
    {
      "id": "grafana.candidate.5fc9b3abbacc8a2ae99a",
      "syntaxRole": "declarative-or-imperative-paragraph",
      "sourceText": "With data now appearing in our rule setup, our next step is to create an [expression](/docs/grafana/v9.0/panels/query-a-data-source/use-expressions-to-manipulate-data/about-expressions/#using-expressions). Move to section `B`. For this scenario, we want to create a Reduce expression that will reduce the above to a single value. In this image, you can see that we have chosen to reduce our time-series data the `Last` value from input `A`. In this case, it returns a value 53 degrees celsius for Tank A5:",
      "headingPath": [
        "How to create Grafana alerts with InfluxDB and the Flux query language",
        "Example 1: Create an alert when a value is above or below a set threshold",
        "Add expressions to your Grafana Alert rule"
      ],
      "source": {
        "path": "docs/sources/tutorials/create-alerts-from-flux-queries/index.md",
        "gitBlobSha": "ff67f50b26681d3dc7d4d8e7eb10e4620c9488c3",
        "sha256": "48b344a4b7311941040db4785da4177ce4cd0dcaccadd5ebdd24c843c328bc40",
        "lineStart": 70,
        "lineEnd": 70,
        "url": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/grafana/grafana/6193dc03311b631b9727b560d24369e683dc396e/docs/sources/tutorials/create-alerts-from-flux-queries/index.md#L70-L70"
      },
      "applicability": {
        "status": "oss-labelled-source-candidate-not-yet-reviewed",
        "products": [
          "enterprise",
          "oss"
        ]
      },
      "atomicity": "unreviewed-may-require-splitting-or-deduplication",
      "acceptanceContract": null,
      "verificationStatus": "not-tested"
    },
    {
      "id": "grafana.candidate.a23b944e88850513bce6",
      "syntaxRole": "declarative-or-imperative-paragraph",
      "sourceText": "Finally, we need to create a math expression that Grafana will alert on. In our case we will write an expression with two conditions separated by the OR `||` operator. We want to trigger an alert any time our result in section `B` is less than 30 or more than 60. This looks like `$B < 30 || $B > 60`:",
      "headingPath": [
        "How to create Grafana alerts with InfluxDB and the Flux query language",
        "Example 1: Create an alert when a value is above or below a set threshold",
        "Add expressions to your Grafana Alert rule"
      ],
      "source": {
        "path": "docs/sources/tutorials/create-alerts-from-flux-queries/index.md",
        "gitBlobSha": "ff67f50b26681d3dc7d4d8e7eb10e4620c9488c3",
        "sha256": "48b344a4b7311941040db4785da4177ce4cd0dcaccadd5ebdd24c843c328bc40",
        "lineStart": 74,
        "lineEnd": 74,
        "url": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/grafana/grafana/6193dc03311b631b9727b560d24369e683dc396e/docs/sources/tutorials/create-alerts-from-flux-queries/index.md#L74-L74"
      },
      "applicability": {
        "status": "oss-labelled-source-candidate-not-yet-reviewed",
        "products": [
          "enterprise",
          "oss"
        ]
      },
      "atomicity": "unreviewed-may-require-splitting-or-deduplication",
      "acceptanceContract": null,
      "verificationStatus": "not-tested"
    },
    {
      "id": "grafana.candidate.779ae4b3f984e333a7a1",
      "syntaxRole": "declarative-or-imperative-paragraph",
      "sourceText": "Set the alert condition to `C - expression`. We can now preview our alert. Here is a preview of this alert when the state is `Normal`:",
      "headingPath": [
        "How to create Grafana alerts with InfluxDB and the Flux query language",
        "Example 1: Create an alert when a value is above or below a set threshold",
        "Add expressions to your Grafana Alert rule"
      ],
      "source": {
        "path": "docs/sources/tutorials/create-alerts-from-flux-queries/index.md",
        "gitBlobSha": "ff67f50b26681d3dc7d4d8e7eb10e4620c9488c3",
        "sha256": "48b344a4b7311941040db4785da4177ce4cd0dcaccadd5ebdd24c843c328bc40",
        "lineStart": 78,
        "lineEnd": 78,
        "url": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/grafana/grafana/6193dc03311b631b9727b560d24369e683dc396e/docs/sources/tutorials/create-alerts-from-flux-queries/index.md#L78-L78"
      },
      "applicability": {
        "status": "oss-labelled-source-candidate-not-yet-reviewed",
        "products": [
          "enterprise",
          "oss"
        ]
      },
      "atomicity": "unreviewed-may-require-splitting-or-deduplication",
      "acceptanceContract": null,
      "verificationStatus": "not-tested"
    },
    {
      "id": "grafana.candidate.8dddcf3bd3fa43edeff2",
      "syntaxRole": "declarative-or-imperative-paragraph",
      "sourceText": "And here is a preview of this alert when the state is `Alerting`:",
      "headingPath": [
        "How to create Grafana alerts with InfluxDB and the Flux query language",
        "Example 1: Create an alert when a value is above or below a set threshold",
        "Add expressions to your Grafana Alert rule"
      ],
      "source": {
        "path": "docs/sources/tutorials/create-alerts-from-flux-queries/index.md",
        "gitBlobSha": "ff67f50b26681d3dc7d4d8e7eb10e4620c9488c3",
        "sha256": "48b344a4b7311941040db4785da4177ce4cd0dcaccadd5ebdd24c843c328bc40",
        "lineStart": 82,
        "lineEnd": 82,
        "url": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/grafana/grafana/6193dc03311b631b9727b560d24369e683dc396e/docs/sources/tutorials/create-alerts-from-flux-queries/index.md#L82-L82"
      },
      "applicability": {
        "status": "oss-labelled-source-candidate-not-yet-reviewed",
        "products": [
          "enterprise",
          "oss"
        ]
      },
      "atomicity": "unreviewed-may-require-splitting-or-deduplication",
      "acceptanceContract": null,
      "verificationStatus": "not-tested"
    },
    {
      "id": "grafana.candidate.39e141b797a2bc72c83e",
      "syntaxRole": "declarative-or-imperative-paragraph",
      "sourceText": "Note that the Reduce expression above is needed. Without it, when previewing the results, Grafana would display `invalid format of evaluation results for the alert definition B: looks like time series data, only reduced data can be alerted on`.",
      "headingPath": [
        "How to create Grafana alerts with InfluxDB and the Flux query language",
        "Example 1: Create an alert when a value is above or below a set threshold",
        "Add expressions to your Grafana Alert rule"
      ],
      "source": {
        "path": "docs/sources/tutorials/create-alerts-from-flux-queries/index.md",
        "gitBlobSha": "ff67f50b26681d3dc7d4d8e7eb10e4620c9488c3",
        "sha256": "48b344a4b7311941040db4785da4177ce4cd0dcaccadd5ebdd24c843c328bc40",
        "lineStart": 86,
        "lineEnd": 86,
        "url": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/grafana/grafana/6193dc03311b631b9727b560d24369e683dc396e/docs/sources/tutorials/create-alerts-from-flux-queries/index.md#L86-L86"
      },
      "applicability": {
        "status": "oss-labelled-source-candidate-not-yet-reviewed",
        "products": [
          "enterprise",
          "oss"
        ]
      },
      "atomicity": "unreviewed-may-require-splitting-or-deduplication",
      "acceptanceContract": null,
      "verificationStatus": "not-tested"
    },
    {
      "id": "grafana.candidate.730ce72cdf5ee328cbbd",
      "syntaxRole": "declarative-or-imperative-paragraph",
      "sourceText": "💡Tip: In case your locale is still stubbornly using Fahrenheit, we can modify the above Flux query by adding (before the aggregateWindow statement) a map() function to to convert (or map) the values from °C to °F. Note that we are not creating a new field. We are simply remapping the existing value.",
      "headingPath": [
        "How to create Grafana alerts with InfluxDB and the Flux query language",
        "Example 1: Create an alert when a value is above or below a set threshold",
        "Add expressions to your Grafana Alert rule"
      ],
      "source": {
        "path": "docs/sources/tutorials/create-alerts-from-flux-queries/index.md",
        "gitBlobSha": "ff67f50b26681d3dc7d4d8e7eb10e4620c9488c3",
        "sha256": "48b344a4b7311941040db4785da4177ce4cd0dcaccadd5ebdd24c843c328bc40",
        "lineStart": 88,
        "lineEnd": 88,
        "url": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/grafana/grafana/6193dc03311b631b9727b560d24369e683dc396e/docs/sources/tutorials/create-alerts-from-flux-queries/index.md#L88-L88"
      },
      "applicability": {
        "status": "oss-labelled-source-candidate-not-yet-reviewed",
        "products": [
          "enterprise",
          "oss"
        ]
      },
      "atomicity": "unreviewed-may-require-splitting-or-deduplication",
      "acceptanceContract": null,
      "verificationStatus": "not-tested"
    },
    {
      "id": "grafana.candidate.3817fc865499360ee78d",
      "syntaxRole": "declarative-or-imperative-paragraph",
      "sourceText": "Using these three steps you can create a Flux-based Grafana Alert that will trigger on either of two thresholds from a single data source. But what if you need to trigger an alert based on **multiple conditions and from multiple time-series**? In example two we will cover this very scenario.",
      "headingPath": [
        "How to create Grafana alerts with InfluxDB and the Flux query language",
        "Example 1: Create an alert when a value is above or below a set threshold",
        "Conclusion"
      ],
      "source": {
