    {
      "id": "grafana.candidate.86f63a08485545c73fda",
      "syntaxRole": "declarative-or-imperative-paragraph",
      "sourceText": "You can add variables and filters to groupings that apply only to the panels in that grouping.",
      "headingPath": [
        "Dashboard panel groupings",
        "Section-level variables and filters"
      ],
      "source": {
        "path": "docs/sources/visualizations/dashboards/build-dashboards/create-dashboard/dashboard-groupings.md",
        "gitBlobSha": "7e666cc7cbb84b63dfee54bf067a6c03e784e7cf",
        "sha256": "c47f46f800915574767d871cfb0f709a00ffbc347d85e18000de024ef2f140d4",
        "lineStart": 265,
        "lineEnd": 265,
        "url": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/grafana/grafana/6193dc03311b631b9727b560d24369e683dc396e/docs/sources/visualizations/dashboards/build-dashboards/create-dashboard/dashboard-groupings.md#L265-L265"
      },
      "applicability": {
        "status": "oss-labelled-source-candidate-not-yet-reviewed",
        "products": [
          "cloud",
          "enterprise",
          "oss"
        ]
      },
      "atomicity": "unreviewed-may-require-splitting-or-deduplication",
      "acceptanceContract": null,
      "verificationStatus": "not-tested"
    },
    {
      "id": "grafana.candidate.57fb7a9592f444b58a8c",
      "syntaxRole": "declarative-or-imperative-paragraph",
      "sourceText": "For example, if your dashboard includes both an API gateway and a database, you might want to apply two different `$instance` variables. Section-level variables and filters for address this by letting each row or tab have its own independent scope. In the API gateway and database scenario, the API gateway grouping can use one set of instances, while a database grouping uses another set. However, both groupings still share the same time range, and the underlying dashboard remains unchanged.",
      "headingPath": [
        "Dashboard panel groupings",
        "Section-level variables and filters"
      ],
      "source": {
        "path": "docs/sources/visualizations/dashboards/build-dashboards/create-dashboard/dashboard-groupings.md",
        "gitBlobSha": "7e666cc7cbb84b63dfee54bf067a6c03e784e7cf",
        "sha256": "c47f46f800915574767d871cfb0f709a00ffbc347d85e18000de024ef2f140d4",
        "lineStart": 267,
        "lineEnd": 270,
        "url": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/grafana/grafana/6193dc03311b631b9727b560d24369e683dc396e/docs/sources/visualizations/dashboards/build-dashboards/create-dashboard/dashboard-groupings.md#L267-L270"
      },
      "applicability": {
        "status": "oss-labelled-source-candidate-not-yet-reviewed",
        "products": [
          "cloud",
          "enterprise",
          "oss"
        ]
      },
      "atomicity": "unreviewed-may-require-splitting-or-deduplication",
      "acceptanceContract": null,
      "verificationStatus": "not-tested"
    },
    {
      "id": "grafana.candidate.22e66538c1b967c1c235",
      "syntaxRole": "declarative-or-imperative-paragraph",
      "sourceText": "The panel query editor is context-aware, so the autocomplete only shows the variables available to the panel you're editing. Also, section-level variables and filters carry over when you convert between rows and tabs, change layouts, and work with repeating rows and tabs. When you ungroup rows or tabs, their section-level variables and filters move up to the next level.",
      "headingPath": [
        "Dashboard panel groupings",
        "Section-level variables and filters"
      ],
      "source": {
        "path": "docs/sources/visualizations/dashboards/build-dashboards/create-dashboard/dashboard-groupings.md",
        "gitBlobSha": "7e666cc7cbb84b63dfee54bf067a6c03e784e7cf",
